# Line endings, so that a clone on Windows still hashes to the pins.
#
# L6 review, I1. Git for Windows installs with `core.autocrlf=true`, so a clone there
# rewrites every LF file in the working tree to CRLF. `CONNECTOR_MANIFEST_SHA256S` and
# `CONNECTOR_DIR_SHA256S` are digests of those bytes, so both tables would mismatch and —
# per STATUS.md — every start would refuse. The same conversion hits the three embedded
# manifest copies (`crates/goalcup-verifier/tests/traces/{opencode,codex}-live/manifest.toml`
# and `tests/fixtures/compliant/manifest.toml`), so V13 would read `manifest_not_pinned` on
# the committed traces too. The promise is a public verifier anyone can run offline, and
# PRD R1 names a Windows service, so this is not hypothetical.
#
# `eol=lf` is the load-bearing half: it forces LF in the WORKING TREE on every platform,
# whatever `core.autocrlf` says.
* text=auto eol=lf

# And these are never converted in either direction. Every `.jsonl` in this repository is
# byte-exact evidence — a chained record stream, a hook capture, a redaction fixture — and
# 63 of the committed ones already hold bytes Git's own text heuristic calls binary. That
# heuristic reads the first 8000 bytes only, so a fixture whose first NUL sits past that
# would be treated as text and rewritten. A digest cannot survive being guessed about.
*.jsonl -text
